{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>\"שפתים ישק משיב דברים נכוחים\" (Mishlei 24:26).</b>" "<b>Shelomo</b> the king, of blessed memory, teaches here (Mishlei 24) about the virtue of the wise man whose words are sweet to people and all desire to hear them, because he speaks upright words and answers every questioner with them. The intent is not that it is fitting to kiss him on the lips — for what would that come to teach? And furthermore, if so, it should have said שפתים יושק — but Scripture rather says: the lips of the listeners kiss him who answers upright words. The intent is to say that the ears of the people are attentive to his words and their hearts are firm and whole with him to hear them, as though he were kissing the lips of the listeners, because he is beloved of his fellows and his words are heard and accepted by them. This is by way of the plain sense (peshat)." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah,</b> שפתים ישק משיב דברים נכוחים: he calls words of wisdom ״דברי נכוחים״ in the manner of what is written there (Mishlei 8:9): ״כלם נכוחים למבין.״ And the expression ישק — because a kiss is physical cleaving, the metaphor comes upon intellectual cleaving, as it says (Shir haShirim 1:2): ״ישקנו מנשיקות פיהו.״ And the word שפתים is from the expression שפת הנהר — 'the lip of the river' — whose meaning is boundary. And the interpretation of the verse is that the wise man who contemplates the principles of the supernal wisdom, from which his thought is never separated, and who answers every questioner with upright words — שפתים ישק — he, in the end, cleaves to the boundaries of that wisdom. And the virtue of this cleaving is מיתת נשיקה — death by a kiss — which is cleaving to the revered Name; and this is the degree of passionate love (חשק) that David, of blessed memory, mentioned (Tehilim 91:14): ״כי בי חשק ואפלטהו.״ And the righteous one who attains this virtue — his death is not by means of the destructive force, as with the rest of the children of the world. Now Moshe and Aharon and Miryam attained it, for Scripture mentioned concerning them (Numbers 33:1) ״על פי ה׳״ — and that is death by a kiss; but in the case of Miryam it did not say ״על פי ה׳״ because it is not the way of supernal dignity, and Rabbi Eliezer said: Miryam too died by a kiss, as it is derived by the verbal analogy of ״שם שם״ from Moshe; and why did it not say ״על פי״? Because it would be a dishonor to say, and so forth. And likewise with the fathers of the world, concerning whom it is written ״בכל מכל כל.״ Also Rabbenu haKadosh, of blessed memory, attained it, for it is clear that since he was elevated in the attainment of wisdom, his death was not by means of the destroying angel; and they said, of blessed memory, at the time of Rabbi's passing, he raised his ten fingers upward and said: Master of the universe, it is revealed and known before You that I toiled with my ten fingers and did not enjoy even my little finger; may it be Your will that there be peace in my resting. A bat kol went forth and said: ״יבא שלום ינוח על משכבו.״ And they said in the Yerushalmi: When Rabbi Yehudah haNasi died, Rabbi Yannai announced: There is no priesthood today. And the reason is that the priests are warned against the impurity of the dead, for they are the ministers of the Most High, consecrated to His service; and since his death was not by means of the destructive force but by the Shekhinah, there was no impurity there. Therefore Rabbi Yannai announced: There is no priesthood today, because the priests were occupied with his burial and the law of priesthood departed from them that day. But the rest of the children of the world, whose death is by means of the destructive force — which is the cause of the impurity of the dead — the priests are warned against this, that they not become impure. And this is what is written." "<b>\"Speak to the priests, the sons of Aharon, and say to them: for a dead person he shall not become impure among his people.\"</b> It says first ״אמור אל הכהנים״ — 'speak to the priests' — and then repeats ״ואמרת אליהם״ — 'and say to them.' Its measure is: ״אמור אל הכהנים״ — the foregoing matter, for they are the teachers of Torah who admonish the people; ״ואמרת אליהם״ — this matter." "<b>And our Rabbis,</b> of blessed memory, interpreted ״אמור ואמרת״ — 'speak and say' — as a warning to the great ones concerning the small ones. And this warning is to say that we should not assist in the impurity of the small ones with our hands, and from this many warnings in the Torah are derived, according to what they, of blessed memory, expounded regarding blood and creeping things and impurity; and from them we learn for all prohibitions in the Torah, that we should not assist in any of them so that the small ones transgress them — but if they do them on their own, we are not commanded to restrain them. And according to this midrash, Scripture says ״אמור אל הכהנים בני אהרן״ — 'speak to the priests, the sons of Aharon' — and repeat and say to them that they shall not become impure, and that all the sons of Aharon shall be careful regarding this, even the small ones." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'אמור ואמרת' (speak and you shall say) — the first utterance: that he must defile himself for a מצוה-met (an unattended corpse for which a priest is obligated to defile himself); the second utterance: that he must not defile himself for others. And the reason Scripture needed to say 'the priests' — when it should have been sufficient to say 'speak to the sons of Aaron' — was to exclude the חללים (priests disqualified by lineage) from the scope of the passage: for although they are sons of Aaron, they are not priests. Therefore it needed to specify 'the priests.'" "<b>The sons of Aaron.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: and not the daughters of Aaron — for the warning against corpse-defilement does not apply to women. The reason for this I wrote in the section of צו: because women brought death to the world. And the reason this passage of priests was juxtaposed to the verse about the אוב and ידעוני — they said in the Midrash: the Holy One, blessed be He, foresaw that Saul was destined to reign over Israel and to kill Nob, the city of the priests, and to seek out an אוב and ידעוני, as is said (I Samuel 28:7): ״ויאמר שאול לעבדיו בקשו לי אשת בעלת אוב ואלכה אליה ואדרשה״; this is what Scripture says (Isaiah 8:19): ״וכי יאמרו אליכם דרשו אל האובות ואל הידעונים המצפצפים והמהגים הלוא עם אל אלהיו ידרוש בעד החיים אל המתים״. He said to them: if the nations of the world say to you, 'Leave Elohim in the heavens and seek out the אובות and the ידעונים,' say to them: 'הלוא עם אל אלהיו ידרוש' — should Israel abandon his Elohim and seek the dead? — as Elijah said to Ahaziah (II Kings 1:6): ״המבלי אין אלהים בישראל אתה שולח לדרוש בבעל זבוב אלהי עקרון״ — why should we seek the dead, the deities of the dead, of whom it is written (Psalms 115:5): ״פה להם ולא ידברו עינים להם ולא יראו״ — 'in behalf of the living,' concerning whom it is written (Jeremiah 10:10): ״הוא אלהים חיים ומלך עולם״. If you ask, from whom shall we seek? — He says (Deuteronomy 17:9): ״ובאת אל הכהנים הלוים ואל השופט אשר יהיה בימים ההם ודרשת״. R. Yehoshua of Sikhnin said in the name of R. Yehoshua ben Levi: the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses every generation and its seekers, every generation and its leaders, every generation and its kings, and He showed him Saul the king — him and his sons falling by the sword. He said before Him: Master of the Universe! The first king who will arise will be run through by the sword? He said to him: 'אמור אל הכהנים בני אהרן' — until here. And in the section of שופטים I shall explain to you, with the help of the Blessed Name, the matter of the woman with the אוב, for there are opinions there and divergent lines of reasoning."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {4 "The reference in [2] is to Numbers 33:1 per the Hebrew citation (במדבר ל״ג:א׳); the verse ״על פי ה׳״ more famously appears at Numbers 33:38 (Aharon) and Deuteronomy 34:5 (Moshe), but the printed citation is normalized to what is written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:58.106639395Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}